[{:seon.task/name "a-private-wait-wakes-for-the-fiber-dump"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-26: kill -INFO on a hung Seon test runner (pid 71239) printed no fiber dump. Its main thread was in a plain thread's read, which jolt.io-poller blocks in a private kqueue of its own (wait-ready on a plain thread: a private kevent); that kqueue has no SIGINFO filter, so the dump's Chez handler never got an event check. The dump (jolt.host/fiber-dump, Jolt d598fc04, docs/forks.md) wakes only a main carrier idle in the poller's own kqueue, which carries EVFILT_SIGNAL for SIGINFO; a test runner, the commonest hang, waits in the private one. Correct, at the cause: the private per-wait kqueue also takes SIGINFO (EVFILT_SIGNAL), so the wait wakes, its thread runs the handler at its event check, and the wait resumes (a signal is no readiness: the loop goes back into kevent). Proof red first: a plain thread blocked reading a pipe nobody writes, kill -INFO to its own process, and the dump's text on stderr while the read still waits (a Jolt unit or smoke case)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.io-poller-test/plain-thread-blocked-reading-pipe-nobody-writes-sent}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fiber-waits-for-a-childs-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the blocking-calls audit of 2026-10-25 (now in docs/failure-points.md; this task holds its open entries). The poller's half is done (Jolt 504b7034): jolt.io-poller/exit-chan pid answers a child's exit as a value on a channel (kqueue NOTE_EXIT on macOS, a pidfd on Linux), and Seon's kq-wait/e13_wait are gone (seon.agent/read-to-exit, seon.fork-server wait with it). Open: Process.waitFor is still a WNOHANG poll with backoff (host/chez/java/process.ss, proc-wait-blocking: 0.2 ms to 10 ms), and seon.lib/wait-status (src/seon/lib.clj) is a blocking waitpid, run after the exit has arrived. Correct, in Jolt (a jolt/<topic> branch): Process.waitFor parks its fiber on the pid's exit (the exit-chan's descriptor) instead of polling, and returns the status that arrival reaps. Replaces the waitFor poll (the fork server already parks on poller/exit-chan, done). Proof to write: a fiber waiting on a child that exits later returns its status while a second fiber keeps running (no carrier held), with no poll timer armed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.io-poller-test/fiber-process-waitfor-child-exits-later-returns-status}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-thread-in-a-forked-child-collects-without-end"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-28 (root: ./seon forks line-parks at ~35 GB RSS, orphaned 8 h, and again as pid 89726). The line-parks fork case started a future (Thread/sleep 200, then a write) in a child forked from a main carrier, whose own fiber then parked in jolt.io-poller/wait-ready on a line-reader. Sampled (the 2026-10-28 evidence (working papers, not kept)): the future's thread in S_do_gc for the whole sample, sweep_continuation and copy_stack copying ever larger continuations, RSS 3.9 GB after 4 s at 99% CPU; the main thread in s_mutex_acquire, waiting on the collector; the run ended after 97-113 s when it ended at all, on main's binary as on this branch. Not the reader: the main thread does not spin. The test no longer starts a thread in the child (its writer is the parent's thread), so this is open in Jolt only. Cause to find: what the future's thread allocates without bound in a forked child (a sleep or a timer that captures continuations on a thread that is not a carrier?). Proof to write: a Jolt gate that forks from a main carrier, starts a future with a sleep in the child while its main fiber parks on a socket, and asserts the child ends with bounded allocation (bytes, not time)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.io-poller-test/child-forked-main-carrier-starts-future-sleep-while}}]
